### ### 5. Selection Sort vs. Insertion Sort

#### Question

Two arrays contain the same number of elements:

`A = [2, 3, 5, 7, 8, 6, 9, 10]`

`B = [10, 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 3, 2]`

Which algorithm - Selection Sort or Insertion Sort - would perform better on each array? Compare them based on:

* Comparisons
* Swaps or shifts
* Adaptability to existing order
* Best- and worst-case time complexity

Can two algorithms with the same worst-case complexity behave very differently in practice?

---

### Solution

Both Selection Sort and Insertion Sort have a worst-case time complexity of `O(n²)`, but their actual performance can be different depending on the arrangement of the elements.

### Array A

`A = [2, 3, 5, 7, 8, 6, 9, 10]`

This array is **almost sorted**. Only `6` is out of its correct position.

#### Insertion Sort

Insertion Sort works well with this type of array because it takes advantage of the elements that are already sorted.

When it reaches `6`, it shifts `8` and `7` to the right and inserts `6` in its correct position.

So, only a small number of shifts are required.

**Therefore, Insertion Sort would perform better on Array A.**

#### Selection Sort

Selection Sort does not take much advantage of the fact that the array is already mostly sorted. It still searches the remaining part of the array to find the minimum element during every pass.

So, it performs almost the same number of comparisons even though the array is nearly sorted.

---

### Array B

`B = [10, 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 3, 2]`

This array is in **reverse order**, so it is a worst-case type of input for Insertion Sort.

#### Insertion Sort

Every new element needs to be moved past almost all the elements before it.

This results in many shifts.

For 8 elements, the maximum number of shifts is:

`1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 6 + 7 = 28`

So Insertion Sort performs a large number of shifts.

#### Selection Sort

Selection Sort still makes about the same number of comparisons:

`7 + 6 + 5 + 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 28`

However, it performs at most one swap in each pass.

So, there are at most **7 swaps**.

**Therefore, Selection Sort would generally be better for Array B**, especially when swaps/shifts are expensive.

---

### Comparison

| Feature                            | Selection Sort | Insertion Sort          |
| ---------------------------------- | -------------- | ----------------------- |
| Array A                            | Less suitable  | **Better**              |
| Array B                            | **Better**     | Less suitable           |
| Comparisons                        | `O(n²)`        | `O(n²)` worst case      |
| Swaps/Shifts                       | Few swaps      | Can require many shifts |
| Works well with nearly sorted data | No             | **Yes**                 |
| Best case                          | `O(n²)`        | **O(n)**                |
| Worst case                         | `O(n²)`        | `O(n²)`                 |

### Can algorithms with the same worst-case complexity behave differently?

**Yes.**

Even though both algorithms have `O(n²)` worst-case time complexity, they do not always perform the same amount of work.

Insertion Sort is **adaptive**, meaning it performs much better when the array is already sorted or nearly sorted. Selection Sort does not benefit much from existing order because it continues searching for the minimum element on every pass.

Therefore:

* For **nearly sorted data**, Insertion Sort is usually better.
* For **reverse or highly unsorted data**, Selection Sort can be preferable when reducing swaps/shifts is important.

So, having the same Big-O complexity does **not** mean two algorithms will have the same practical performance.

