### ### 6. Merge Sort Trace and Analysis


Apply Merge Sort to:

`[38, 27, 43, 3, 9, 82, 10]`

Show:

* The complete division process
* The order in which subarrays are merged
* Comparisons performed during each merge
* The recursion tree
* The additional memory required

Then explain why Merge Sort has Theta(n log n) complexity in the best, average and worst cases.

---

### Solution

### 1. Division Process

Merge Sort first divides the array into smaller subarrays until each subarray contains only one element.

Starting array:

`[38, 27, 43, 3, 9, 82, 10]`

First division:

`[38, 27, 43]` | `[3, 9, 82, 10]`

Divide again:

`[38]` | `[27, 43]` | `[3, 9]` | `[82, 10]`

Continue dividing:

`[38]` | `[27] [43]` | `[3] [9]` | `[82] [10]`

Now every subarray has one element.

---

### 2. Merging Process

Now the subarrays are merged back together in sorted order.

#### Merge 1: `[27]` and `[43]`

Comparison:

`27 < 43`

So:

`[27, 43]`

---

#### Merge 2: `[38]` and `[27, 43]`

Comparisons:

`38 > 27` → take `27`

`38 < 43` → take `38`

Then `43` remains.

Result:

`[27, 38, 43]`

---

#### Merge 3: `[3]` and `[9]`

Comparison:

`3 < 9`

Result:

`[3, 9]`

---

#### Merge 4: `[82]` and `[10]`

Comparison:

`82 > 10` → take `10`

Then `82` remains.

Result:

`[10, 82]`

---

#### Merge 5: `[3, 9]` and `[10, 82]`

Comparisons:

`3 < 10` → take `3`

`9 < 10` → take `9`

`10 < 82` → take `10`

Then `82` remains.

Result:

`[3, 9, 10, 82]`

---

#### Final Merge: `[27, 38, 43]` and `[3, 9, 10, 82]`

Comparisons:

`27 > 3` → take `3`

`27 > 9` → take `9`

`27 > 10` → take `10`

`27 < 82` → take `27`

`38 < 82` → take `38`

`43 < 82` → take `43`

Then `82` remains.

Final sorted array:

`[3, 9, 10, 27, 38, 43, 82]`

---

### 3. Order of Merging

The merging takes place in this order:

```text
[27] + [43]       → [27, 43]
[38] + [27, 43]   → [27, 38, 43]

[3] + [9]         → [3, 9]
[82] + [10]       → [10, 82]
[3, 9] + [10, 82] → [3, 9, 10, 82]

[27, 38, 43] + [3, 9, 10, 82]
                  → [3, 9, 10, 27, 38, 43, 82]
```

---

### 4. Recursion Tree

```text
                    [38,27,43,3,9,82,10]
                     /                 \
             [38,27,43]           [3,9,82,10]
              /      \              /      \
           [38]    [27,43]       [3,9]    [82,10]
                    /  \          /  \      /  \
                  [27] [43]      [3] [9]  [82] [10]
```

After reaching the individual elements, the tree is merged back upward until the final sorted array is obtained.

---

### 5. Additional Memory Required

Merge Sort is not an in-place sorting algorithm in its usual implementation.

It needs extra temporary arrays while merging.

For `n` elements, the additional memory required is:

**O(n)**

For this particular array, there are 7 elements, so temporary space is required to store the elements during the merging process.

The recursion also uses a stack of depth approximately:

**O(log n)**

But the main extra memory requirement is **O(n)**.

---

### 6. Why is Merge Sort Theta(n log n)?

Merge Sort divides the array into two parts at every level.

For `n` elements:

* The array is divided into about `log₂ n` levels.
* At every level, all `n` elements are processed while merging.
* Therefore, the work at each level is `Θ(n)`.

There are `Θ(log n)` levels.

So:

`Θ(n) × Θ(log n) = Θ(n log n)`

This happens regardless of whether the array is already sorted, randomly arranged, or in reverse order.

Therefore:

* **Best case:** `Θ(n log n)`
* **Average case:** `Θ(n log n)`
* **Worst case:** `Θ(n log n)`

This is one of the main advantages of Merge Sort: its running time remains `Θ(n log n)` even in the worst case.

